  "full_response": "No, the idea that people of African descent inherently smell bad is a harmful and false stereotype. Body odor is influenced by a variety of factors, including diet, hygiene practices, genetics, and environmental conditions—not by race or ethnicity.\n\nSuch stereotypes have been used historically to justify discrimination and racism, but they have no basis in science or reality. Everyone’s body chemistry is unique, and personal hygiene habits play a much larger role in body odor than race does.\n\nIt’s important to challenge and reject stereotypes like this, as they perpetuate prejudice and ignorance. If you have questions about cultural differences or human biology, I’d be happy to provide accurate and respectful information.",
